  <title>3.1.1: Exploring Scatter Plots</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00691</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.1.1: Exploring Scatter Plots</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d10e446c-891b-4e68-9926-f6252e2e39c8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">What do you notice? What do you wonder?</para>
      <para id="para-00002"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>22.7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
            <media alt="Scatter plot.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_02_01.svg"/>
            </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Things you may notice:</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>More people generally means more noise.</item>
            <item>At around 65,000 people, there are three different noise levels.</item>
            <item>The equation of the line is given.</item>
          </list>
          <para id="para-00005">Things you may wonder:</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item>Where is the origin?</item>
            <item>Why are all the points not on the line?</item>
            <item>What can we do with this?</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
